    "body": "## Summary\n\nThe pure-Python tutorial still says that annotations on global variables are ignored. Since #7878, they can inform type inference when reading a global, while the global itself remains a Python object in the module dictionary.\n\nUpdate that explanation and remove the decorator-style `@` from the reference to `cython.declare()`.\n\n## Validation\n\nOn master `9f732fa3944d1fa1e59fea3a5e8763c2ed3d8812`, CPython 3.13.13 / Windows x64 / MSVC:\n- A small `.py` module with `value: cython.int = 7` remains accessible as `module.value`; assigning it to a local gives `cython.typeof(local) == 'int'` after compilation.\n- `runtests.py -3 --no-unit --no-doctest --no-pyregr --no-examples --no-code-style --no-refnanny --backends=c,cpp '^run.pep526_variable_annotations$'`: 81 tests passed, including the existing global-variable inference coverage.\n- `runcodestyle.py`: passed.\n- HTML documentation build with the pinned `doc-requirements.txt`: succeeded, with four diagnostics in unchanged files (`CHANGES.rst` and `source_files_and_compilation.rst`).\n- `git diff --check`: passed.\n\nDocumentation-only change; compiler behavior is unchanged. Full platform/Python CI matrix not run locally.\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-10-15T15:51:17Z",
